CloudEndure will jump in to
help as well at that point.

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:23.740
You could also work on
disaster recovery workloads

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:25.390
with CloudEndure.

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:27.190
So it's not just migrating.

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:30.040
It's also making sure that if
you have a disaster-- if you

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:35.340
need a disaster recovery system,
you could set up that as well.

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:37.180
Touch on Velostrata
a little bit--

00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:40.360
if you have a
VMware machines that

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:42.880
were virtual machines that
you would like to bring over

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:47.410
into Cloud, we just acquired
a company called Velostrata.

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:51.130
And you could do
similar to CloudEndure.

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:55.510
You could bring your VM
instances in virtual--

00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:58.504
VMware virtual machines
into Cloud as well.

00:12:58.504 --> 00:12:59.420
CORI PEELE: Oh, great.

00:12:59.420 --> 00:12:59.980
Yes.

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:02.860
Those of you who are
on 5.5 for VMware

00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:04.600
definitely take note here.

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:05.987
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:13:05.987 --> 00:13:07.570
And then the data
transfer appliance--

00:13:07.570 --> 00:13:11.620
any application you try to move
is going to have some component

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:13.320
of data, is going to have--

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:15.080
it could be petabytes of data.

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:17.030
It could be small
amounts of data.

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:19.480
If you have a large
amount of data

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:22.030
that you want to move
over, we recommend

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:25.240
you use Data Transfer
Appliance, which is really

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:27.070
a box that gets shipped to you.

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:29.380
And then you load the data
out from your data center.

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:30.880
And then you ship it back to us.

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:35.140
And then you see it all in
the Cloud console showing up

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:35.950
magically.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:37.158
CORI PEELE: Oh, that's great.

00:13:37.158 --> 00:13:39.580
There's a lot of time for
those migration projects

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:41.940
where you calculate
the network bandwidth

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:44.774
and realize it's going to
take maybe (LAUGHING) weeks.

00:13:44.774 --> 00:13:45.940
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Or years.

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:47.150
CORI PEELE: Or years, yes.

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:50.415
So this is definitely a way
to speed up the process.

00:13:50.415 --> 00:13:51.540
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:51.910
Yeah.

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:53.410
And then there are
some other tools

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:56.170
if you just want to move
data from other clouds

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:57.340
into our cloud.

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:59.230
There are some online
tools, as well,

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:01.135
that we offer transfer services.

00:14:04.095 --> 00:14:06.160
So here's what I
was talking about.

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:12.400
Lush is a cosmetic company,
and they are really--

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:13.910
it's an e-commerce company.

00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:17.940
They make handmade cosmetics,
soaps, and shampoos.

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:23.740
We were able to bring them
over into Google Cloud,

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:28.385
migrate their services into
Google Cloud in 22 days.

00:14:28.385 --> 00:14:30.760
CORI PEELE: That's great for
a global platform, isn't it.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:31.690
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:32.380
Yeah.

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:35.500
So that's why I wanted
to bring a story out,

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:38.860
just because we
could relate to it.

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:41.590
And that speaks a lot about
all the professional services

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:45.021
offerings that we discussed.

00:14:45.021 --> 00:14:45.520
All right.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:50.090
So with that, second step,
hybrid and multi cloud--

00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:54.700
all right, so I talk with
the infrastructure leads

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:57.460
and with the IT
leads all the time,

00:14:57.460 --> 00:15:01.570
and I find that the longer an
enterprise has been around,

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:04.900
the more complex their
systems start to get

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:07.120
and infrastructure
starts to get.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:10.120
Over the years, they've
created applications

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:13.150
that are on premise, some
that are in other clouds,

00:15:13.150 --> 00:15:18.010
some that are SaaS applications,
and all of those kind you

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:19.730
have to work together.

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:26.140
So it becomes this huge mesh of
things that sometimes gets out

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:27.760
of control.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:31.510
And sometimes it
becomes so tightly

00:15:31.510 --> 00:15:36.350
coupled with each other
that it's inflexible.

00:15:36.350 --> 00:15:40.270
And then sometimes
it's so unsecure

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:42.830
that it's just flying all over.

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:47.000
And you kind of lose
control, over time.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:52.060
So due to this tight coupling,
every time a new application

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:56.770
request comes in, an IT
manager or infrastructure lead

00:15:56.770 --> 00:16:00.580
is, like, it's going to
take x number of days

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:02.410
and x number-- not days.

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:03.340
It's probably months.

00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:06.970
[LAUGHING] x amount
of months, and this

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:10.660
is much cost until we actually
add this one tiny feature.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:12.130
CORI PEELE: One--
yes, one feature.

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:14.165
Imagine when you have
M&amp;A activity or--

00:16:14.165 --> 00:16:15.290
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:16.998
CORI PEELE: --you
know, you get acquired.

00:16:16.998 --> 00:16:18.690
And you're like, oh
great, but now what?

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:19.930
How do you get
everything together?

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:21.054
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:16:21.054 --> 00:16:25.630
So what if-- and "what
if" is the theme today.

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:28.670
What if there was a
way to avoid this?

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:34.070
The industry has converged to
a set of open-source standards.

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:38.990
And those standards
are there to address

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:42.250
this issue of tight coupling.

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:47.080
Companies have found that fully
open modern approaches, based

00:16:47.080 --> 00:16:50.050
on containers, offer
a flexible approach

00:16:50.050 --> 00:16:55.000
to dealing with infrastructure
and tight coupling.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.340
Kubernetes is an
open source platform

00:16:57.340 --> 00:17:01.150
for automating deployment
of applications

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:03.850
onto the infrastructure.

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:07.780
And then it provides you
the portability and ability

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:10.569
to change and
redeploy wherever you

00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:13.000
want to take that application.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:17.200
So it basically decouples
your underlying infrastructure

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:18.910
from your application.

00:17:18.910 --> 00:17:21.010
So whatever the
infrastructure is,

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:23.440
if you decide to
change it tomorrow,

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:26.240
2 years from now,
10 years from now,

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:30.040
you're not locked down
into that infrastructure.

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:30.790
CORI PEELE: Great.

00:17:30.790 --> 00:17:32.710
Avoiding lock-in and
making sure you're

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:35.005
open, flexible, but also secure.

00:17:35.005 --> 00:17:36.130
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:36.640
CORI PEELE: Yeah.

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:37.640
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:42.100
And you're basically
master of your own destiny

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:46.480
I would like to say, just
because you made the decision

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:52.900
that I don't want to be tightly
locked with either one cloud

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:57.760
provider or with my on-premise
systems or anything else.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:00.655
I can just take this application
over and put it there tomorrow.

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:06.430
And then, when you
work with Google,

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:10.000
Google offers the advanced
fully-managed environment

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:10.960
for Kubernetes.

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:15.220
So that's the advantages
you get with working

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:18.430
with Google Container
Engine or Kubernetes engine

00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:22.000
because you basically just
leave all the management

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:24.960
and maintenance of your
Kubernetes clusters

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:29.704
onto Google, and you go
build the application.

00:18:29.704 --> 00:18:30.370
CORI PEELE: Yes.

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:30.870
Yes.

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:33.630
So Greek for
helmsman, making sure

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:35.890
you-- we actually
do above and beyond

00:18:35.890 --> 00:18:39.730
because we are able to
adopt the latest versions.

00:18:39.730 --> 00:18:41.736
So those of you who
heard us say it's

00:18:41.736 --> 00:18:43.360
really easy with
Kubernetes, but you've

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:45.730
been working on
your local machines

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:49.630
and building a lot of things
yourselves, yes, try out

00:18:49.630 --> 00:18:51.295
the Google Kubernetes engine.

00:18:51.295 --> 00:18:52.420
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:53.800
Actually, you know what?

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:59.290
There is a GitHub page
by Kelsey Hightower,

00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:01.540
and he compares
the two together.

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:03.610
Like, you do Kubernetes
on your own--

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:06.520
I think he calls it
"Kubernetes the Hard Way,"

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:09.940
and then you do the same
thing on Kubernetes engine,

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:13.240
and then you see the comparison,
like, how it's day and night.

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:15.171
[CHUCKLING]

00:19:15.171 --> 00:19:15.670
All right.

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:19.810
So we're moving to the third
stop in our journey, which is

00:19:19.810 --> 00:19:22.720
modernizing your application.

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:27.160
I have a lot of conversations
with leaders of application

00:19:27.160 --> 00:19:28.390
development teams.

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:31.546
And they all say pretty
much the same thing

00:19:31.546 --> 00:19:32.920
that you're seeing
on the screen.

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:36.080
Deployment agility
is what they want.

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:38.650
They want the ability to
get to the market, time

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:42.460
to market for their features
and applications to be small,

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:45.550
and then they want
to do all of that

00:19:45.550 --> 00:19:48.310
without compromising security.

00:19:48.310 --> 00:19:50.245
So in one way,
shape, or form, those

00:19:50.245 --> 00:19:52.344
are the things that come out.

00:19:52.344 --> 00:19:53.010
CORI PEELE: Yes.

00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:54.551
And this is, again,
traditional, what

00:19:54.551 --> 00:19:56.370
you think of software
application firms,

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:58.580
but even firms
designing hardware.

00:19:58.580 --> 00:19:59.580
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:02.160
CORI PEELE: So at the Design
Automation Conference,

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:05.910
we had people talking about
maybe using Google Cloud

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:08.790
because it helps them
with their global teams,

00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:11.850
globally distributed
teams, and really

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:16.770
helps their team advance a
lot quicker and a bit more

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:17.400
manageable.

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:19.100
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Yeah, exactly.

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:19.600
OK.

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:22.290
So that's where
their challenges are,

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:25.410
the development and application
development teams' challenges

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:26.090
are.

00:20:26.090 --> 00:20:29.640
And achieving these goals
is obviously difficult

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:35.160
and is made more difficult when
you depend on legacy systems.

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:39.300
Because despite whatever
speed you want to go with,

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:42.510
those legacy systems
are things that we

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:45.600
need to understand
and figure out

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:48.900
how you're going to
break the barrier

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:50.887
and move forward
with your speed.

00:20:50.887 --> 00:20:51.720
CORI PEELE: Exactly.

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:53.610
Hopefully it's
documented, and the person

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:55.890
who built it is still
around, but sometimes that

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:57.825
may not be there, but
you still depend on it.

00:20:57.825 --> 00:20:58.950
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:20:58.950 --> 00:21:01.675
CORI PEELE: So now this
is what we can do to help.

00:21:01.675 --> 00:21:02.800
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:07.860
So you have a gap
between the need

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:11.730
to deliver the customer
experiences quickly and fast,

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:15.810
and the flexibility that's
offered by your legacy systems.

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:18.940
Now, how do you bridge that gap?

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:21.340
What if we said that
you can actually

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:26.830
bridge that gap without ripping
any of your legacy systems?

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:30.680
You can actually solve
that by API management,

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:33.490
which you see on this screen.

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:37.480
Apigee is Google's API
management platform,

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:41.260
and it helps bridge that gap
between your legacy systems

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:46.210
and the need to deliver great
customer experiences quickly

00:21:46.210 --> 00:21:48.790
and fast-- get to market.

00:21:48.790 --> 00:21:51.790
It basically streamlines
how you get your--

00:21:51.790 --> 00:21:56.260
how you get access to the APIs,
how your developers get access

00:21:56.260 --> 00:22:00.880
to the APIs, how
you build your APIs,

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:04.420
and then how you
monitor them, manage

00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:08.530
them, make them accessible
to your partners--

00:22:08.530 --> 00:22:10.180
not just your
internal employees,

00:22:10.180 --> 00:22:12.070
but your partners,
your customers--

00:22:12.070 --> 00:22:13.774
in a secure fashion.

00:22:13.774 --> 00:22:14.440
CORI PEELE: Yes.

00:22:14.440 --> 00:22:14.940
Yes.

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:18.100
And this is great for
protecting your legacy systems

00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:21.190
because as soon as you have
a partner or some customers

00:22:21.190 --> 00:22:23.880
demanding access to
key functionality,

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:26.350
let's say for your
new chatbot system,

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:28.720
well, you may have
this legacy customer

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:31.690
system in the back end,
and it can't necessarily

00:22:31.690 --> 00:22:34.570
handle the same scale as
all these new systems.

00:22:34.570 --> 00:22:38.890
So yes, Apigee can help throttle
and protect those resources.

00:22:38.890 --> 00:22:39.890
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:40.556
CORI PEELE: Yes.

00:22:40.556 --> 00:22:43.840
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: And then,
for those that are actually--

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:45.940
that actually don't
have an interface

00:22:45.940 --> 00:22:49.390
to be accessed by anybody--
like, I want to say access

00:22:49.390 --> 00:22:50.950
this mainframe system.

00:22:50.950 --> 00:22:55.000
And it has got all this great
data, but there's no API.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:59.380
I can't give it to anybody,
because I have this huge amount

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:01.390
of data, and it's locked.

00:23:01.390 --> 00:23:02.710
And I have no access--

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:04.280
no way to get it out.

00:23:04.280 --> 00:23:08.250
And that's where also Apigee
can help, where you can--

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:10.960
they can help you
build a facade layer

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:13.450
on top of whatever
this old system is

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:16.030
and make it look like
it's a really nice

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:20.250
and shiny RESTful API that
got-- that just got built.

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:20.952
[CHUCKLING]

00:23:20.952 --> 00:23:21.660
CORI PEELE: Yeah.

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:22.620
Yes, excellent.

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:25.110
So yeah, great for
legacy in addition

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:30.060
to new systems standardizing on
the APIs and developer portals.

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:31.060
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:23:31.060 --> 00:23:33.020
CORI PEELE: Great.

00:23:33.020 --> 00:23:35.500
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: The example
that I want to bring up here

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:41.170
is the Chile implementation
of the national API-based

00:23:41.170 --> 00:23:42.340
architecture.

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:45.670
And what they did here was
Chile had been struggling

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:51.040
and had tried to bring these old
health care and medical records

00:23:51.040 --> 00:23:52.660
systems together.

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:55.000
And because they were
legacy in nature,

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:57.460
they had spent a lot
of time and money,

00:23:57.460 --> 00:24:01.150
but didn't get the results
because the data was

00:24:01.150 --> 00:24:01.900
locked down.

00:24:01.900 --> 00:24:05.830
They couldn't get
it all together.

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:08.800
They used the API
management platform, Apigee,

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:11.080
and what they were
able to do was

00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:17.500
get APIs in front of every
single medical records system.

00:24:17.500 --> 00:24:25.420
And now physicians and people
like normal users in Chile,

00:24:25.420 --> 00:24:29.540
who were requiring health care
support were able to access it.

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:31.600
And the partners
in the ecosystem

00:24:31.600 --> 00:24:33.790
were able to access
that information

00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:35.560
and build better systems.

00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:37.580
So it's a great blog.

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:39.190
Go ahead; read it.

00:24:39.190 --> 00:24:43.415
It totally goes with
this whole health

00:24:43.415 --> 00:24:47.890
care non-native digital story.

00:24:47.890 --> 00:24:50.080
CORI PEELE: Yes, it's
great for critical systems

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:52.860
and transformation, the
public sector level.

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:53.860
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:24:53.860 --> 00:24:56.477
CORI PEELE: Very,
very compelling case.

