that ML isn't possible without
proper data collection.

00:25:11.540 --> 00:25:13.810
So you can dream about ML.

00:25:13.810 --> 00:25:16.570
But you won't be able
to execute anything

00:25:16.570 --> 00:25:20.810
if you don't have a
proper data strategy.

00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:25.450
So in the first
three steps, you'll

00:25:25.450 --> 00:25:30.060
be dealing with data
decisions like in migration,

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:32.770
in hybrid, and then
in modernizing.

00:25:32.770 --> 00:25:36.280
You'll be dealing with a lot
of data-related decisions

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:40.450
with databases-- which
databases that I need?

00:25:40.450 --> 00:25:41.740
Where should I put my data?

00:25:41.740 --> 00:25:43.690
What is it that I
actually collect.

00:25:43.690 --> 00:25:45.550
And then how to store--

00:25:45.550 --> 00:25:48.580
so you can analyze
the cost effectively.

00:25:48.580 --> 00:25:52.570
So when you make
all those decisions,

00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:58.510
that's a better way to move
into the direction of ML and AI.

00:25:58.510 --> 00:25:59.350
CORI PEELE: Yes.

00:25:59.350 --> 00:26:02.020
So acquiring and
preparing the data

00:26:02.020 --> 00:26:04.690
so you understand
what's necessary,

00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:08.980
and then it also helps deal with
some of these machine learning

00:26:08.980 --> 00:26:11.680
fairness activities, and a lot
of other research companies

00:26:11.680 --> 00:26:14.050
are getting into now-- so
a great way to manage that.

00:26:14.050 --> 00:26:14.430
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:26:14.430 --> 00:26:15.400
CORI PEELE: Sounds good.

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:17.525
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: And
almost all analytics leaders

00:26:17.525 --> 00:26:19.590
we meet with struggle
with the challenge

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:22.950
of scaling their infrastructure,
finding the critical talent

00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:26.820
that can help with actually
working on the data and data

00:26:26.820 --> 00:26:29.460
analytics initiatives,
and then improving cost,

00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:33.570
and managing the growing
expectations from the business

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:38.880
leaders around getting valuable
insights from their data.

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:43.410
So many organizations
also recognize

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:45.210
that artificial
intelligence will

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:48.670
create competitive
advantages for them.

00:26:48.670 --> 00:26:53.330
And some are already starting
to drive AI/ML initiatives.

00:26:53.330 --> 00:26:56.370
However, they
explore very quickly

00:26:56.370 --> 00:27:01.410
that their current data, or
the current data warehouse,

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:06.090
may not be able to provide
the needed speed at which they

00:27:06.090 --> 00:27:08.730
would be successful
at AI and ML.

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:16.350
So now the question is, are
you ready for AI and ML?

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:19.770
If your company is
not good at analytics,

00:27:19.770 --> 00:27:21.690
it's not ready for AI.

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:25.530
Sorry to say this, but you need
to be really good at analytics

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:30.240
first before you start
thinking about AI.

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:32.040
You need to know
what your data is.

00:27:32.040 --> 00:27:33.360
You need to analyze it.

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:37.650
You need to first be good
at analytics, period.

00:27:37.650 --> 00:27:41.650
If you're not doing that today,
that's where you need to start.

00:27:41.650 --> 00:27:48.452
So that's your stepping stone
into starting to do ML and AI.

00:27:48.452 --> 00:27:49.160
CORI PEELE: Yeah.

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:51.330
So for a governance
standpoint, how

00:27:51.330 --> 00:27:53.400
do you know you're
good at analytics?

00:27:53.400 --> 00:27:56.490
Well, if you have different
teams doing calculations

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:59.910
for the same metric, and you
get different answers sometimes,

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:02.940
then maybe you want
to improve there.

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:05.095
And there may be a
process to go through.

00:28:05.095 --> 00:28:06.220
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:28:06.220 --> 00:28:07.320
Yeah.

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:12.000
We recently observed a company
embracing a big data database

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:17.180
like Hadoop architecture
to move towards AI/ML.

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:19.380
But they found that
adding clusters

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:24.210
to keep up with the data volume
in the query data requests

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:29.320
became increasingly
difficult and expensive.

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:32.310
So there are things that you
keep learning over time as you

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:33.930
start to move into--

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:38.970
move beyond that analytics
aspect of things and data.

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:46.320
I'm actually going skip this
one in the interest of time.

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:50.060
So Google offers a fully
managed petabyte-scale,

00:28:50.060 --> 00:28:53.090
low-cost data
warehousing solution--

00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:58.220
a platform where you can ingest
the data at terabyte-scale

00:28:58.220 --> 00:29:01.160
through an offering
called Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:29:01.160 --> 00:29:02.780
which is a messaging service.

00:29:02.780 --> 00:29:05.210
It has the ability
to take requests

00:29:05.210 --> 00:29:09.500
from millions and millions
of different places

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:13.040
and things and devices
and IoT things,

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:16.520
and just get it
inside your system,

00:29:16.520 --> 00:29:20.870
ingest it in your system,
and keep it as messages.

00:29:20.870 --> 00:29:23.360
And then there is the
Reliable Data Pipeline,

00:29:23.360 --> 00:29:28.875
which can take streaming and
batch imports at the same time

00:29:28.875 --> 00:29:31.090
called Cloud Data Flow.

00:29:31.090 --> 00:29:33.785
And then, as you
move along, you need

00:29:33.785 --> 00:29:36.800
a data warehousing platform.

00:29:36.800 --> 00:29:45.080
And we have BigQuery, which is a
petabyte-scale data warehousing

00:29:45.080 --> 00:29:45.680
platform.

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:48.560
I like to say it's
SaaS, because you really

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:50.660
are just running queries.

00:29:50.660 --> 00:29:53.540
And you don't have to
worry about whatever

00:29:53.540 --> 00:29:56.690
amount of infrastructure is
required behind the scenes

00:29:56.690 --> 00:30:01.910
to actually run that query
and get the results back.

00:30:01.910 --> 00:30:03.740
And then, when
you're ready for it,

00:30:03.740 --> 00:30:07.730
you have Cloud ML engine
for predictive insights.

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:10.430
And then a data visualization.

00:30:10.430 --> 00:30:13.892
You could visualize data
within Google Data Studio--

00:30:13.892 --> 00:30:14.600
CORI PEELE: Free.

00:30:14.600 --> 00:30:15.890
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: --for free.

00:30:15.890 --> 00:30:19.110
Or if you have a data
visualization platform

00:30:19.110 --> 00:30:21.920
of your choice, we have
connectors from BigQuery

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:25.850
that can just connect the
data into your visualization

00:30:25.850 --> 00:30:27.080
platform as well.

00:30:27.080 --> 00:30:33.920
So that's the data platform that
we've built that-- we really

00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:38.030
believe in petabyte-scale
data platform that

00:30:38.030 --> 00:30:40.430
is low-cost and serverless.

00:30:40.430 --> 00:30:43.070
So most of our products
in that category

00:30:43.070 --> 00:30:44.840
are serverless, so you
don't have to worry

00:30:44.840 --> 00:30:47.915
about the infrastructure.

00:30:47.915 --> 00:30:48.860
All right.

00:30:48.860 --> 00:30:51.290
So we went through a lot.

00:30:51.290 --> 00:30:53.630
So let's summarize
this very quickly.

00:30:53.630 --> 00:30:58.130
We've walked through migration
of your legacy system

00:30:58.130 --> 00:31:01.670
into Google Cloud using
professional services--

00:31:01.670 --> 00:31:06.850
Cloud Start, Cloud Sprint,
maybe Cloud Secure--

00:31:06.850 --> 00:31:10.700
And some tools, like
CloudEndure, Velostrata,

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:12.080
transfer appliance.

00:31:12.080 --> 00:31:15.520
And once you are migrated
or migrated partly,

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:21.430
you're in that hybrid
and multi cloud scenario

00:31:21.430 --> 00:31:23.870
where you want to make sure
you build your applications

00:31:23.870 --> 00:31:26.750
in a containerized fashion so
you can take them anywhere,

00:31:26.750 --> 00:31:29.270
and you're not locked down.

00:31:29.270 --> 00:31:32.270
And then, moving into
modernizing your applications

00:31:32.270 --> 00:31:35.900
using Apigee API management,
where you could unlock

00:31:35.900 --> 00:31:38.240
that potential of your
own mainframe data,

00:31:38.240 --> 00:31:41.300
or old data that's sitting
somewhere that's not

00:31:41.300 --> 00:31:44.450
accessible, we use Apigee
API management platform

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:46.460
to build that facade
layer and make

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:49.280
it accessible to other
developers, partners

00:31:49.280 --> 00:31:51.830
that can unlock that potential.

00:31:51.830 --> 00:31:56.030
And then you move into data and
analytics and machine learning.

00:31:56.030 --> 00:32:00.350
And we talked about Google's
petabyte-scale serverless data

00:32:00.350 --> 00:32:03.400
management platform for that.

00:32:03.400 --> 00:32:04.400
CORI PEELE: Yes.

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:06.500
And so for those who
are just trying to get

00:32:06.500 --> 00:32:09.680
started, and you're
responsible for the budget,

00:32:09.680 --> 00:32:12.350
we can definitely
help-- custom engineers,

00:32:12.350 --> 00:32:14.330
professional services,
and our partners

00:32:14.330 --> 00:32:18.030
to do TCO analysis
using additional tools.

00:32:18.030 --> 00:32:20.975
So there's a lot to
learn, and it's great.

00:32:20.975 --> 00:32:22.850
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: And
we wish you good luck.

00:32:22.850 --> 00:32:26.630
And if you would like
to get in touch with us,

00:32:26.630 --> 00:32:30.050
reach out to either
your customer team,

00:32:30.050 --> 00:32:33.740
or go on to
cloud.google.com, and you

00:32:33.740 --> 00:32:36.222
will find all the
resources there as well.

00:32:36.222 --> 00:32:37.430
CORI PEELE: Great, thank you.

00:32:37.430 --> 00:32:42.277
And stay tuned for a live
Q&amp;A in less than a minute.

00:32:42.277 --> 00:32:42.860
We'll be back.

00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:17.550
Welcome back.

00:35:17.550 --> 00:35:20.400
And now we'll get started with
the live Q&amp;A. Thanks a lot

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:22.030
for your questions.

00:35:22.030 --> 00:35:25.620
So first, "Our executives
think that cloud is not

00:35:25.620 --> 00:35:27.300
secure for our systems.

00:35:27.300 --> 00:35:29.370
We deal with financial
services, and all the data

00:35:29.370 --> 00:35:31.140
is very confidential.

00:35:31.140 --> 00:35:34.249
How would you help me make
an argument for cloud?"

00:35:34.249 --> 00:35:36.540
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: That's an
interesting question-- one

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:41.040
that we get very often.

00:35:41.040 --> 00:35:45.900
The way I would say that
is, cloud is actually

00:35:45.900 --> 00:35:48.741
much more secure than your
physical data centers.

00:35:48.741 --> 00:35:49.990
CORI PEELE: Google Cloud, yes.

00:35:49.990 --> 00:35:51.114
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:35:51.114 --> 00:35:53.160
Well, yeah-- our cloud.

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:53.991
Google Cloud.

00:35:53.991 --> 00:35:56.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:56.820 --> 00:35:59.160
And the reason I
say that is based

00:35:59.160 --> 00:36:02.445
on a couple of different things.

00:36:02.445 --> 00:36:05.130
When we store your
data, it's encrypted

00:36:05.130 --> 00:36:07.200
at rest and in motion.

00:36:07.200 --> 00:36:09.330
You have the choice to
bring your own keys.

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:13.290
You have the choice to have
Google manage your keys.

00:36:13.290 --> 00:36:18.630
And so, however strong your
policies around keys are,

00:36:18.630 --> 00:36:22.750
we can totally accommodate that.

00:36:22.750 --> 00:36:26.700
But even if you don't, we
are encrypting your data

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:32.010
at rest and in motion whenever
it within the Google Cloud

00:36:32.010 --> 00:36:33.240
platform.

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:37.500
And then, second, we're
actually not just--

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:40.920
and the reason I said Google
Cloud data centers, and when

00:36:40.920 --> 00:36:43.940
you store data in Google
Cloud it's much more secure,

00:36:43.940 --> 00:36:47.740
is because you would
take your data--

00:36:47.740 --> 00:36:50.340
and imagine this is your data.

00:36:50.340 --> 00:36:53.760
Actually, imagine
this is your data

00:36:53.760 --> 00:36:59.340
and you put it in your data
center, physical location,

00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:00.400
and you encrypt it.

00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:00.970
Fine.

00:37:00.970 --> 00:37:05.190
It's probably going to be secure
until somebody hits your data

00:37:05.190 --> 00:37:09.190
center and tries to access it.

00:37:09.190 --> 00:37:10.920
But if it is in
Google Cloud, we've

00:37:10.920 --> 00:37:16.230
actually shredded that data
into multiple different pieces.

00:37:16.230 --> 00:37:19.080
And those pieces are
encrypted individually.

00:37:19.080 --> 00:37:22.770
And then they are shipped
to different data center

00:37:22.770 --> 00:37:25.360
locations, and then
they are stored there.

00:37:25.360 --> 00:37:28.680
So even if somebody was
able to access or hack

00:37:28.680 --> 00:37:32.400
one of our data centers
and get that one piece,

00:37:32.400 --> 00:37:35.730
I highly doubt that somebody is
able to access all of our data

00:37:35.730 --> 00:37:38.310
centers and put all
of that data together

00:37:38.310 --> 00:37:41.040
from the shredded
pieces and make it back

00:37:41.040 --> 00:37:44.907
into that one piece and
be able to decrypt it.

00:37:44.907 --> 00:37:45.740
CORI PEELE: Exactly.

00:37:45.740 --> 00:37:47.073
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: It's highly--

00:37:47.073 --> 00:37:48.600
I find it highly improbable.

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:53.440
So that's why I say Google
Cloud is much more secure.

00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:57.610
And yeah, I would-- if
you need more information,

00:37:57.610 --> 00:38:01.800
we have whitepapers
on how we encrypt data

00:38:01.800 --> 00:38:04.600
and what our strategy
around data encryption is

00:38:04.600 --> 00:38:06.100
and what are your
different options.

00:38:06.100 --> 00:38:08.069
So please do check
that out as well.

00:38:08.069 --> 00:38:08.735
CORI PEELE: Yes.

00:38:08.735 --> 00:38:10.350
And as custom
engineers, I know we

00:38:10.350 --> 00:38:13.540
love giving security
briefings to our customers.

00:38:13.540 --> 00:38:14.850
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:38:14.850 --> 00:38:17.370
If you feel like you would
like somebody from our team

00:38:17.370 --> 00:38:20.924
to come in and do a briefing
for your executives,

00:38:20.924 --> 00:38:23.340
one of our team members would
be happy to do that as well.

00:38:23.340 --> 00:38:24.850
CORI PEELE: OK, great.

00:38:24.850 --> 00:38:27.690
And next, "We have a
few legacy systems.

00:38:27.690 --> 00:38:30.360
Payroll and ERP are
the main systems.

00:38:30.360 --> 00:38:32.550
We have designed
these internally,

00:38:32.550 --> 00:38:34.500
and we are having to
spend a lot of money

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:36.150
maintaining them over the years.

00:38:36.150 --> 00:38:37.989
How can Cloud help?"

00:38:37.989 --> 00:38:40.530
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So there are
a couple of different options

00:38:40.530 --> 00:38:42.180
here.

00:38:42.180 --> 00:38:46.950
I think I'll say three
to touch on different--

00:38:46.950 --> 00:38:50.100
and there could be more or
a combination of the ones

00:38:50.100 --> 00:38:51.640
that I talk about.

00:38:51.640 --> 00:38:56.130
One is you could just
migrate your entire ERP

00:38:56.130 --> 00:39:00.010
system into cloud--
obviously, a huge undertaking,

00:39:00.010 --> 00:39:01.960
but that could be one option.

00:39:01.960 --> 00:39:12.680
The other is you basically
do a part migration.

00:39:12.680 --> 00:39:15.830
Bring parts of your
system into cloud.

00:39:15.830 --> 00:39:20.540
And then the third, which
in some cases works out,

00:39:20.540 --> 00:39:24.470
is the SaaS offering, right?

00:39:24.470 --> 00:39:28.460
If you are not using all the
systems, all the features

00:39:28.460 --> 00:39:30.770
and functionalities
of your legacy system,

00:39:30.770 --> 00:39:33.360
or you were looking for
something where you're, like,

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:36.200
I don't have the people to
maintain this old system.

00:39:36.200 --> 00:39:40.100
I would rather pay a
subscription-based model,

00:39:40.100 --> 00:39:43.250
you can actually also
choose a SaaS-based service

00:39:43.250 --> 00:39:45.920
for payroll or ERP
and just not have

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:49.304
to worry about maintaining it.

00:39:49.304 --> 00:39:50.720
CORI PEELE: Yeah,
so that's great.

00:39:50.720 --> 00:39:53.390
And then, next,
"What is the benefit

00:39:53.390 --> 00:39:57.050
of a multi-cloud or
hybrid cloud strategy?"

00:40:00.172 --> 00:40:01.880
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: We
talked a little bit

00:40:01.880 --> 00:40:05.710
about this, which is
containers, right?

00:40:05.710 --> 00:40:13.100
So when you think about
multi cloud or hybrid cloud,

00:40:13.100 --> 00:40:16.300
you're basically in the
arena where you're thinking--

00:40:16.300 --> 00:40:18.519
or you've been on
premise for a long time,

00:40:18.519 --> 00:40:20.560
or you've been in another
cloud for a long time--

00:40:20.560 --> 00:40:22.090
and you're thinking,
I can't reap

00:40:22.090 --> 00:40:25.540
the benefits of every single
thing that's out there.

00:40:25.540 --> 00:40:28.630
And I want to actually
take advantage of that.

00:40:28.630 --> 00:40:32.600
Every single cloud has
different strengths.

00:40:32.600 --> 00:40:35.030
If I talk about Google Cloud,
we talked about container

00:40:35.030 --> 00:40:36.520
and Kubernetes engine.

00:40:36.520 --> 00:40:41.760
We are the best at offering you
a managed Kubernetes offering.

00:40:41.760 --> 00:40:47.470
So if you were to deal with
Kubernetes and containers,

00:40:47.470 --> 00:40:49.735
you would probably look to us.

00:40:49.735 --> 00:40:54.010
And then, similarly, taking
an example of BigQuery.

00:40:54.010 --> 00:41:01.450
If you want to just run queries
on petabyte storage space

00:41:01.450 --> 00:41:04.210
and not have to worry about
the thousands of servers

00:41:04.210 --> 00:41:06.580
that you need to
actually run that query,

00:41:06.580 --> 00:41:09.070
you probably look to Google.

00:41:09.070 --> 00:41:13.030
And so the reason I give these
examples-- every single cloud

00:41:13.030 --> 00:41:15.200
has their own advantages.

00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:20.500
So the customers that we speak
with who want to go multi cloud

00:41:20.500 --> 00:41:24.070
want to reap the benefits
of all the different cloud

00:41:24.070 --> 00:41:27.490
providers have to offer and
don't want to be locked down.

00:41:27.490 --> 00:41:30.790
So that is why moving
towards containers

00:41:30.790 --> 00:41:34.480
is an approach that
keeps you not locked down

00:41:34.480 --> 00:41:37.630
into one particular
cloud provider

00:41:37.630 --> 00:41:40.410
and keeps you open for
all the possibilities

00:41:40.410 --> 00:41:43.839
that your business
might open up later.

00:41:43.839 --> 00:41:45.130
CORI PEELE: Yeah, that's great.

00:41:45.130 --> 00:41:48.250
Google has their really
big open philosophy.

00:41:48.250 --> 00:41:50.860
And also, as you see
with our commitment

00:41:50.860 --> 00:41:53.855
to open source, when we have
a great idea like Kubernetes,

00:41:53.855 --> 00:41:54.730
we make it available.

00:41:54.730 --> 00:41:57.175
And now other cloud providers
are able to interoperate.

00:41:57.175 --> 00:41:58.300
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:41:58.300 --> 00:42:00.820
CORI PEELE: And so now,
"Legacy ERP systems

00:42:00.820 --> 00:42:03.940
are running on physical
servers with SAN attached.

00:42:03.940 --> 00:42:05.620
How can one cross-check
the performance

00:42:05.620 --> 00:42:06.972
before migrating to Cloud?"

00:42:06.972 --> 00:42:08.680
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
You want to take that?

00:42:08.680 --> 00:42:10.192
CORI PEELE: Yes.

00:42:10.192 --> 00:42:14.290
Because I worked with a lot
of different legacy systems

00:42:14.290 --> 00:42:18.130
and also did a lot of work
as a middleware architect,

00:42:18.130 --> 00:42:21.120
so this comes up a lot.

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:23.980
But you first need to think
about the requirements

00:42:23.980 --> 00:42:26.911
of your particular ERP system.

00:42:26.911 --> 00:42:28.660
So most of the time,
you don't necessarily

00:42:28.660 --> 00:42:32.050
have limitations from
a license standpoint.

00:42:32.050 --> 00:42:34.930
But from the Google Cloud
Platform perspective,

00:42:34.930 --> 00:42:37.720
we offers several
different options

00:42:37.720 --> 00:42:40.960
for compute and
storage, including

00:42:40.960 --> 00:42:45.520
one of our recently released
beta offerings of our Cloud

00:42:45.520 --> 00:42:46.810
Filestore.

00:42:46.810 --> 00:42:52.090
So in addition to
SSD and hard disk,

00:42:52.090 --> 00:42:54.930
we do have various
storage options.

00:42:54.930 --> 00:42:56.950
But I think might be
even more interesting

00:42:56.950 --> 00:43:01.020
is if, you are an SAP customer,
go to the Google Cloud

00:43:01.020 --> 00:43:04.570
Launcher in the Console--

00:43:04.570 --> 00:43:06.670
cloud.google.com.

00:43:06.670 --> 00:43:12.670
Search for SAP, and then do
a click to deploy for SAP.

00:43:12.670 --> 00:43:14.950
And then test the performance.

00:43:14.950 --> 00:43:16.790
Check the versioning
to see if it matches,

00:43:16.790 --> 00:43:18.650
and then test the performance.

00:43:18.650 --> 00:43:22.720
So the best way for you to check
is to do a proof of concept.

00:43:22.720 --> 00:43:25.240
And we make it very simple.

00:43:25.240 --> 00:43:28.650
Again, you can deploy SAP
with a couple of clicks.

00:43:28.650 --> 00:43:30.790
I think probably less
than five clicks.

00:43:30.790 --> 00:43:34.010
So try that, and
then let us know.

00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:36.100
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
All right, awesome.

00:43:36.100 --> 00:43:36.850
CORI PEELE: Great.

00:43:36.850 --> 00:43:39.700
So, stay tuned for
the next session,

00:43:39.700 --> 00:43:45.910
How G Suite Customers use the
